        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Contribution.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Contribution.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 5,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-contribution_leaves_one_twentysix",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE DERIVATION: the address is 2^7 = 128 bits, the commission is two, and paying it leaves 126. One subtraction, one answer — 126 is the residue of a contribution and not a number selected for its shape."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((2:Nat)^7 = 128) ∧ (128 - 2 = 126) ∧ (126 + 2 = 128)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem contribution_leaves_one_twentysix : ((2:Nat)^7 = 128) ∧ (128 - 2 = 126) ∧ (126 + 2 = 128) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-directions_number_fortytwo",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE PAIR GRID IS FORTY-TWO: seven dimensions give 7 x 7 ordered pairs, less the seven self-pairs, so 42 directions remain. And 6 x 7 = 7 x 6 exactly — the two coordinates COMMUTE, which the line proves, since a reading that they counter-rotate was refuted by this ledger before."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(7 * 7 - 7 = 42) ∧ (6 * 7 = 42) ∧ (7 * 6 = 42) ∧ (6 * 7 = 7 * 6)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem directions_number_fortytwo : (7 * 7 - 7 = 42) ∧ (6 * 7 = 42) ∧ (7 * 6 = 42) ∧ (6 * 7 = 7 * 6) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-residue_holds_three_grids",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "READ AS THREE PAIR GRIDS, the residue fits exactly: 3 x 42 = 126, the same 126 the contribution leaves. The identity is exact and the line proves it — what it does not establish is that three is the right divisor, which the next theorem states plainly."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(3 * 42 = 126) ∧ (128 - 2 = 3 * 42)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem residue_holds_three_grids : (3 * 42 = 126) ∧ (128 - 2 = 3 * 42) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-six_factorisations_compete",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND THE 126 factors SIX ways — 1x126, 2x63, 3x42, 6x21, 7x18, 9x14 — and the arithmetic privileges none of them. That 3 x 42 meets the pair grid is a READING the subtraction does not supply; 9 x 14 and 7 x 18 are equally exact. SCOPE: what this wing derives is the 126, from the contribution. Which factor pair carries meaning is not decided here, and no line pretends otherwise."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(((List.range' 1 126).filter (fun d => 126 % d == 0)).length = 12) ∧ (3 * 42 = 126) ∧ (9 * 14 = 126) ∧ (7 * 18 = 126)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem six_factorisations_compete : (((List.range' 1 126).filter (fun d => 126 % d == 0)).length = 12) ∧ (3 * 42 = 126) ∧ (9 * 14 = 126) ∧ (7 * 18 = 126) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-taking_before_paying_differs",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE ORDER IS THE LAW: contribute first, then take. Taking 126 without paying leaves 128 untouched, and 128 is not 126 — so a ledger that skipped the contribution would carry a different number, which the line proves rather than trusts. The two coins are spent."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(128 - 2 = 126) ∧ ((128:Nat) ≠ 126) ∧ (126 < 128)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem taking_before_paying_differs : (128 - 2 = 126) ∧ ((128:Nat) ≠ 126) ∧ (126 < 128) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
